sections.body:
By default, Jackson constructs the target object via its no-argument constructor and then populates it via setters (or direct field access via reflection, depending on configuration), matching each JSON key to a Java property of the same name, case-sensitively. Two behaviors here are extremely common sources of confusion, and both matter more than they first appear: first, an unrecognized JSON key -- one that doesn't correspond to any property on the target class -- is silently *ignored* by default, because Spring Boot's Jackson autoconfiguration explicitly disables Jackson's `FAIL_ON_UNKNOWN_PROPERTIES` feature (which, note, is actually *enabled* in plain, non-Boot Jackson -- this is one of the deliberate leniency defaults Spring Boot applies on top of vanilla Jackson). Second, and separately, a JSON key that is simply *missing* from the payload leaves the corresponding Java field at whatever its default value would be -- `null` for objects, `0`/`false` for primitives -- because that's just normal object construction; nothing about a missing key is treated as special or invalid on its own.

```java
public class CreateOrderRequest {
    private String customerName;
    private List<String> items;
    // getters and setters
}
```

POSTing `{"customerName": "Amy", "items": ["mug"]}` against a `@RequestBody CreateOrderRequest` parameter populates both fields correctly. POSTing `{"custName": "Amy", "items": ["mug"]}` -- a typo -- deserializes *successfully*, with no exception at all: `custName` is an unrecognized property and gets ignored, and `customerName` simply stays `null`, because nothing in the payload ever set it.

